WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.319
(birds chirping)

00:00:02.319 --> 00:00:04.450
(water whooshing)

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:05.320
(soft music)

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.302
- The longest border in the
world is kind of strange.

00:00:08.302 --> 00:00:10.160
(soft music)

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:12.630
It goes on for thousands
and thousands of miles,

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:13.810
in like a straight line.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.160
And there's a bunch of weird
little details about this line

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.620
that I'm becoming quite curious about.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.048
This is the border of the
United States and Canada.

00:00:22.048 --> 00:00:24.465
(soft music)

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.550
Yeah I know, two Canada videos in a row.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.210
I'm just really into Canada lately.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.410
And Canada has got some
weird stuff going on

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.420
and it's kind of just
like neighbor to the north

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.770
that no one ever talks about

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:38.430
because it's seemingly
just not that interesting,

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.160
but it actually is really interesting.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.170
That's what I'm here to tell you.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:42.518
So let's do this.

00:00:42.518 --> 00:00:44.650
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.138
- How is it looking up there, Dr. Chris?

00:00:47.138 --> 00:00:47.971
(upbeat music continues)

00:00:47.971 --> 00:00:50.580
I spent the weekend in Vancouver, BC

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.150
hanging out with some friends.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.970
My friends, Chris and
Lizzie and Becky and Chris

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.250
who are just gems of people.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.300
Really fun to hang out with,

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.930
very talented creators.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.550
Iz, what are we up to? Where are we?

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.130
What's going down? You can say it loud.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.249
Izi say it loud.

00:01:06.249 --> 00:01:07.082
(crosstalk)

00:01:07.082 --> 00:01:08.498
- We're getting ramen,
but I'm gonna say it

00:01:08.498 --> 00:01:09.331
- Okay ready, ready.

00:01:09.331 --> 00:01:10.850
- [Host] Okay go
- Great, three, two, one.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.700
- [All] We're getting ramen.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.750
- That worked out really well.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.438
And there was lots of FPV drones.

00:01:17.438 --> 00:01:20.021
(upbeat music)

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.260
- Did you just crash it?

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.399
- I'm very far away.

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:24.882
(air whooshing)

00:01:24.882 --> 00:01:25.715
Do you hear it?

00:01:25.715 --> 00:01:26.710
- And while on this trip,

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.060
one of the things I did
while we were driving around

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.290
is what I always do,

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:33.150
which is, pull out my
phone and started looking

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.403
at the map of where we were.

00:01:35.403 --> 00:01:38.030
(gentle music)

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.200
- And I just started to get
really curious about this line,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.990
this straight line that goes
on for thousands of kilometers.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:47.370
It's like 6,500 kilometers
worth of line on the map.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.350
And I've always just ignored
this line. It's kind of like,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.130
Oh, that's just like the boring line

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.100
that separates these two countries

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.060
that are like best friends

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.600
and have no tension, no drama

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.990
or nothing interesting about this line.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:00.000
It's just straight and boring
is what I always thought.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.053
Then the rabbit holes began.

00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:04.510
(upbeat music)

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.470
There's actually some
really interesting stuff

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.700
about this line like weird enclaves

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:11.460
and a Skype border patrol

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.390
and all this stuff

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.930
that I'll explain in just a second.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:15.410
But first let's remember why I'm here.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.131
I came to explore and to have
a good time with my friends.

00:02:19.131 --> 00:02:22.406
(upbeat music)

00:02:22.406 --> 00:02:24.629
(crosstalk)

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:27.212
(upbeat music)

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.180
Okay, so back to this line.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.390
There's a whole history here.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.800
I don't know if you're interested

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.770
in the history lesson right now. I'm not.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:36.860
So let's just put it this way.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.776
A lot of Canada used to
be owned by the French,

00:02:38.776 --> 00:02:41.210
(typewriter clicking)

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.380
Fought some Wars with Britain,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.250
they lost the wars and
they were kind of like,

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.930
nah, we'll just like
kind of surrender Canada.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.950
French writer, Voltaire
kind of hated Canada

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.970
said it was "Just a few acres of snow"

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:54.270
So the British take over Canada,

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.310
the United States is starting
to expand to the South.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.150
They wanna take over tons of land.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.200
They want to go north
and take over Canada,

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.787
but the British don't really
want them to take over Canada

00:03:01.787 --> 00:03:05.280
and they fight and they end
up not taking over Canada.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.850
And eventually they establish a line

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.570
between north and south that says,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.680
this is Canada and this is United States.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.070
Through a bunch of treaties,

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.333
they determine exactly
where the line's gonna be.

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:19.720
(gentle music)

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.350
There's one number that
completely determined

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.650
how this border would look.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.800
And that number is actually the same name

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.726
as this coffee shop that we're
in right now in Vancouver,

00:03:29.726 --> 00:03:32.893
(gentle upbeat music)

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.333
Forty Ninth Parallel.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.175
(gentle upbeat music)

00:03:42.175 --> 00:03:44.340
(dry leaves cracking)

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.330
This is not stable.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.700
I had this big vision
of sitting on this log.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.699
I don't think it's gonna work.

00:03:49.699 --> 00:03:51.300
(exhales deeply)

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.590
Am I contributing to erosion
with all of this traipsing

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:55.100
through the woods?

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.220
Here they go. They've got a line.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.053
They've got a number,

00:03:57.053 --> 00:03:58.428
the 49th Parallel.

00:03:58.428 --> 00:04:01.080
(soft music)

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.200
This is like the 1800s,
they don't have GPS.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.060
In fact they don't have any precise tools

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.550
for actually measuring
what that line looks like

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.380
when you're in the mountains of Canada.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.640
So they send a bunch of
people out with string.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.140
They made a bunch of straight
lines that were supposed

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.570
to go along the 49th Parallel.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:20.910
And I mean, they did
a pretty darn good job

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.350
for just like using string and compasses.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.530
I'm actually pretty impressed,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.000
but not surprisingly,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.400
if you zoom into this very straight line,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.100
you'll see that there's
actually a little bit

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.660
of zigzagging going on.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.960
The border doesn't actually go perfectly

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.220
along the 49th Parallel.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.210
To demarcate the border,

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.730
they put thousands of these stone markings

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.160
and then they also, to make it very clear,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.440
cut a gap in the trees
that's like 20 feet wide.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.120
It's kind of a bizarre-looking thing,

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:47.180
especially from space.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.312
You just see this like gap in the trees.

00:04:49.312 --> 00:04:51.895
(upbeat music)

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.880
To write the end of it,
continental United States,

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.440
is gonna be south of the 49th Parallel

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.410
and Canada would be north
of the 49th Parallel.

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:03.350
This is a great thing when
you're looking wide at a map,

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:07.090
but nature isn't super
good at straight lines,

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.340
which is why when we draw borders,

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.340
it kind of clashes sometimes with

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.160
how the world actually works.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.877
And so you get some weird stuff

00:05:14.877 --> 00:05:16.940
(upbeat music)

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.200
First over here in Vancouver,
zoom into Vancouver,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.300
and you'll see this little peninsula.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.460
This little peninsula is great,

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.060
just like any other peninsula.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.710
But look what happens when you draw

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.513
the 49th Parallel through it.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.660
This is Point Roberts.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.900
It's a little enclave that
belongs to the United States

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.920
because it's South of the 49th Parallel.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.148
But you can see that it's
like, should be in Canada.

00:05:41.148 --> 00:05:42.970
(upbeat music)

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:44.410
It's cut off from the US.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.410
This little town

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:48.800
of like 1500 people is strange because

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.880
there are people living
there who are US citizens.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.952
They belong to the State of Washington.

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:55.420
(gentle upbeat music)

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.020
They have a little elementary
school in this village,

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.580
but what they don't have is anything

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:00.490
above an elementary school.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.640
So anyone who wants to go to
school has to get on a bus,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:08.210
go across the border into
Canada all the way around

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.780
like 40 kilometers or something like that.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:13.070
Back in the United States, go to school.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.840
And then back across into Canada

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.490
and back into the United States.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:18.220
It's like four border
crossings in one day.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:19.918
Sounds like a nightmare.

00:06:19.918 --> 00:06:22.440
(upbeat music)

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.060
Listen, I know we're talking
about the US-Canada border

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.330
and everything but just take a minute

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.390
to look at this mossy green forest

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.780
that I'm in north of Vancouver.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.060
It's just the most magical
thing I've ever seen.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.103
- I'm really know far away

00:06:36.103 --> 00:06:36.936
(both laughing)

00:06:36.936 --> 00:06:38.603
- [Lady] So far away

00:06:39.816 --> 00:06:40.916
- Can you hear it?

00:06:40.916 --> 00:06:43.499
(upbeat music)

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.520
- [Host] Is everyone convinced that moss

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.860
is the greatest thing in the world yet.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:49.760
- Yeah, of course.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:51.510
- [Host] On the record?
- I was on board.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.060
- [Host] Yeah, you've been
on board since the beginning.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.050
- I was on board from the begins.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:56.397
- [Host] You're on board now, hater.

00:06:56.397 --> 00:06:57.230
- I'm on board.

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:58.063
- [Host] I've got everyone on board.

00:06:58.063 --> 00:06:59.182
- Listen, (indistinct) the whole time

00:06:59.182 --> 00:07:00.411
- Shut up Becky.

00:07:00.411 --> 00:07:02.620
(upbeat music)

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:03.850
- Okay. So back to this line,

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:05.690
keep going down the straight line.

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:07.590
And eventually you'll hit an area where

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:08.700
the whole straight line,

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.910
49th Parallel thing doesn't really work

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:12.750
as a place to draw border.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:17.220
The treaty says that as soon
as this 49th parallel hits

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.280
this Lake, it's called Lake of The Woods

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:20.370
that the border would need

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.830
to trace the Northwest corner,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.330
meaning the upper left
corner of this border,

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:27.870
and then continue onward.
But there was a problem.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.750
The map they were using
to draw this border

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:34.170
was kind of bad and inaccurate.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:38.930
And so it made the lake
look a lot more round

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.160
and cleaner and like more in line

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.900
with their vision for the border.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:44.770
But then when they went
to actually survey it,

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:48.230
they realized that this northwest
corner of the upper left

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.035
was actually way up here,

00:07:51.035 --> 00:07:53.940
(gentle music)

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:58.450
making the border have to
go up to fulfill the terms

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.690
of the treaty and then continue onward.

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.860
From there it starts to follow rivers and

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:04.260
it goes into The Great Lakes.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:05.780
And it's a little more complicated,

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:09.010
but in the process of going
up to fulfill the treaty

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:11.260
and hitting the upper left of this Lake,

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.423
you get this,

00:08:13.423 --> 00:08:15.110
(gentle upbeat music)

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.570
this little blob of land
is appropriately named

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:19.930
The Northwest Angle.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.470
It's the only piece of the
continental United States

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.313
that is north of the 49th Parallel.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.570
There's a couple hundred
people who live there

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.960
and if they want to get in
and out of their community,

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.280
they have to go to this little booth

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:37.370
and they have to video-call like Skype

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.450
the border patrol to like report

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:40.850
that they're coming in and out

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.227
and declare whatever stuff
they wanna bring in and out.

00:08:43.227 --> 00:08:45.040
That's so cool.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.290
It's like Skype border patrol.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:48.876
Sounds cool. I should do that someday.

00:08:48.876 --> 00:08:50.870
(upbeat music)

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.540
We went into this sea-plane in Vancouver

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.604
and we flew over all of these islands.

00:08:58.604 --> 00:09:00.190
(upbeat music)

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.610
And I started to realize
that while it works

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:04.640
to draw these really straight lines in

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:06.560
the middle of Canada,

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.850
when you get to the east
and the west coast in

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:13.370
the Canada region, you start
to get a lot of little islands,

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.470
lots of water weaving
in and out of violins,

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:16.840
cutting them all up.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:19.370
And that's hard to draw lines through.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:20.910
Luckily on the west coast,

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.800
they were able to not stick
with the 49th Parallel,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.670
which would have cut Vancouver Island,

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.680
just in a weird place. They
dipped it down and whatever.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.010
But over on the east
side, they didn't do that.

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:33.725
And you end up with this line.

00:09:33.725 --> 00:09:36.308
(upbeat music)

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.500
This is Canada, but the only way

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.390
to get into Canada from here is through

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:46.343
this land bridge that goes
through the United States.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.650
Kinda weird, probably
not that big of a deal.

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.410
In fact, none of these
are actually a big deal.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.210
Like they're all like kind
of goofy, weird things,

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:58.510
but they don't actually cause
like too much inconvenience.

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:01.430
Although the high schoolers
having to go through

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:04.340
four border crossings every
day is actually pretty weird

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:06.110
and a huge inconvenience.

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:07.260
So I take back what I just said.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.040
So it was a great weekend in Vancouver.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.960
I loved hanging out with
these wonderful people

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.830
and I learned a little
something about this big,

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.220
straight line that I
didn't know anything about.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:21.420
And it confirmed to me
something that I've learned

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.253
time and time again,

00:10:22.253 --> 00:10:26.160
which is some lines on the
map look really simple.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.910
And they just look like straight lines,

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:29.840
but you zoom in enough,

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:31.700
you'll always find weird stuff.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:33.430
So it's always interesting to zoom in

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:35.750
that's I guess the moral of the story.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:37.544
Anyway, thanks for watching.

00:10:37.544 --> 00:10:40.610
(upbeat music)

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.730
Alright. I'm gonna do an
ad now about something

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:46.400
that I care a lot about, which is Audible.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:51.127
I listen to a lot of Audible
and I have since 2011

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:55.620
as this giant library
